A configuration that forbids a pattern you would otherwise reach for by default -- a
`for`/`for...of` loop, a generator, a forward reference to a function defined later in the
file, mixing `import` with `module.exports` in the same file -- is asking for the equivalent
logic written the way this repository's own existing code already writes it (an array method
in place of a loop, functions ordered before their first use, one module system per file), not
for the rule suppressed or the idiom left in place for a formatter to clear -- none of these
are stylistic, and `--fix` corrects none of them. Default to `const` for a binding you never
reassign, and declare it in its own statement -- a binding that turns out to need `let` after
the fact is easier to widen than one this repository's own gate did not narrow for you.

A test that hangs the whole process is not always a connection left open: a mocked hook or
module whose return value feeds a dependency list -- `useEffect`, `useCallback`, `useMemo`, or
an equivalent elsewhere in this ecosystem -- must return the same values across calls. A mock
that returns a new function or object identity every render reproduces the exact infinite
re-render loop a real bug would, and hangs the test runner rather than failing it cleanly.
